  <h2>Rebuild viewpoint instead of backshifting mechanically</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p>After a past reporting verb, English often backshifts a tense or modal because the reporter is looking back from a later point: <span class="ex">is → was</span>, <span class="ex">has finished → had finished</span>, <span class="ex">will → would</span>, <span class="ex">can → could</span>, and <span class="ex">may → might</span>. Past Perfect, <span class="ex">would</span>, <span class="ex">could</span>, <span class="ex">should</span>, and <span class="ex">might</span> commonly remain unchanged.</p>
  <p>Backshift is not automatic. A current reporting verb normally keeps the original viewpoint: <span class="ex">The notice says the branch is open.</span> After a past reporting verb, a speaker may retain a present form for a fact verified as still current: <span class="ex">The inspector said the west branch is safe</span>. A backshifted form can present the information from the past report without telling us whether it remains true: <span class="ex">The inspector said the branch was safe.</span> Choose according to the intended time relationship, not a memorized arrow.</p>
  <p>Reference words also follow meaning. If the reporter is still in the same place and day, <span class="ex">here</span> and <span class="ex">today</span> may remain. If the reporting situation changes, use a calendar date or a clear relative expression. <span class="ex">Must</span> often becomes <span class="ex">had to</span> for an expired past obligation, but it can remain <span class="ex">must</span> when it expresses a deduction or a rule that still applies.</p>
  <div class="practice" data-choice-gap-drill data-success-message="Correct. You chose current and later viewpoints, explicit calendar reference, future-in-the-past, an expired obligation, an unchanged deduction, modal possibility, and a same-place reference.">
