  <h2>Read a meeting record without changing its force</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Fictional Riverton cooling-center record</h3>
    <p>The planning meeting took place on Tuesday, June 11. A June 7 certificate states that the west branch cooling system had passed inspection. The certificate covers the cooling system, not evening security or staffing.</p>
    <p>Chair Maya Chen asked, <span class="ex">“Can the west branch stay open until 9:00 during heat alerts?”</span> Facilities coordinator Omar Bell replied, <span class="ex">“The system passed inspection, but I forgot to schedule the evening security staff.”</span> Omar accepted that omission. He did not say that he had changed the inspection date, and he later denied doing so.</p>
    <p>Safety officer Lena Ortiz said, <span class="ex">“Do not extend the hours until two guards are confirmed.”</span> Community liaison Priya Shah asked the communications team, <span class="ex">“Please publish the hours in Spanish and English by Friday.”</span> Finance manager Eli Brooks said, <span class="ex">“The approved pilot budget can fund two guards for six heat-alert nights in July.”</span></p>
    <p>The resident panel proposed text alerts and recommended that the city add a shaded waiting area. Director Nia Foster said, <span class="ex">“You have my word. I’ll send the final schedule tomorrow.”</span> The final schedule was sent on Wednesday, June 12. It approved six July nights, required two guards, and listed bilingual web and entrance notices. It did not approve a shaded waiting area; that proposal remained under review.</p>
  </div>
